[158.95761, "i", "cat >> README.md <<'EOF'\n\n## Resumability and recovery\n\nEach checkpoint records the last committed document ID, document count, completion\nflag, and committed byte length of each shard. The worker fsyncs every written\nshard (including rotated shards) and its directory **before** atomically replacing\nand syncing the checkpoint. Resume starts strictly after the committed ID; source\nIDs need not be contiguous. A SIGTERM/SIGINT/SIGHUP requests a stop between documents,\nand the processed prefix of the current batch is committed before exiting 75.\n\nIf SIGKILL or a crash interrupts a commit, startup truncates shard tails and removes\nnew shards not covered by the last checkpoint before processing any more documents.\nRecovery is itself safe to interrupt. Missing committed bytes are reported as an\nerror rather than silently skipped. Status reads only checkpointed byte ranges, so\nin-flight or torn tails do not affect displayed progress. As before, use the\nsupervised `./jobs reindex` entry point to serialize writers.\n\nLe"]
[158.957721, "i", "gacy checkpoints lack durable shard offsets and may describe lost buffered rows;\nthey are rebuilt once. The JSONL row format and document transformation are unchanged.\nA completed run is a no-op on subsequent invocations. Use `./jobs clean` followed by\n`./jobs reindex` to intentionally rebuild after the source corpus changes.\n\nFor a one-off backfill, the supported `ops/overrides/reindex.json` can temporarily\nraise `budget_seconds` and/or lower `cooldown_seconds`; the supervisor records the\nactual limits used. Remove that override after completing the backfill. Normal\nproduction limits remain in `ops/limits.json`.\nEOF\n./jobs history; cat var/supervisor.log\n"]
[158.957736, "o", "cat >> README.md <<'EOF'\r\n"]
